design/complexity warn [review] — A function SHOULD NOT exceed the limit of its role in lines, statements, cyclomatic complexity, cognitive complexity or nesting depth, and SHOULD NOT be a dependency hub or a high fan-out coordinator. Cyclomatic complexity is one path plus each `if`, `else if`, loop, error handler clause, non-default switch arm and boolean operator (`&&`, `||`). The remedy is simpler code or a concept that is reused, never the same body moved elsewhere. Mechanical checks SHOULD be preferred for directly expressible rules; heuristic signals SHOULD remain advisory and use deliberately high thresholds.
  expected:
    package sample

    func total(a, b int) int {
        sum := a

        sum += b

        return sum
    }
  evidence: callees=["a","b c"] fan_in=2 fan_out=14 symbol=jit::compile#function
  internal/jit/compile.go:350:1 function coordinates 14 collaborators in its package 95c1bd4
design/errors info (authored warn) [review] — Dependency identity MUST be preserved when callers depend on it; context added to an error MUST keep the cause reachable: an error MUST be compared with `errors.Is`, asserted with `errors.As` and wrapped with `%w`. Semantic errors MUST use stable, named error values or types, and semantic categories MUST NOT be created from ad hoc formatted messages alone. Error categories MUST be translated only at their owning boundary. Errors MUST NOT expose private or sensitive process state. Panic MUST be limited to impossible programmer errors, `Must*`-style APIs, or documented hot-path invariants with one recovery boundary; normal runtime failures MUST return errors.
  expected:
    #[derive(PartialEq)]
    pub enum Failure {
        Missing,
    }

    pub fn is_missing(err: &Failure) -> bool {
        *err == Failure::Missing
    }
